Sure! To compare the declaration flags of your two objects, you’ll want to pass their **stringified** forms to the `areDeclarationFlagsIdentical` function. Here’s a quick example of how you can do that:

```ts
// Suppose you have two objects:
const parameterObject = {
  name: "foo",
  type: "number",
  optional: true,
  visibility: "public",
  async: false,
  abstract: false,
  readonly: false,
  static: false
};

const variableDeclarationObject = {
  name: "bar",
  type: "number",
  optional: true,
  visibility: "public",
  async: false,
  abstract: false,
  readonly: false,
  static: false
};

// Convert them to JSON strings (or any string representation you prefer)
const left = JSON.stringify(parameterObject);
const right = JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject);

// Call the comparison function
const result = areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({ left, right });

console.log(result); // true or false depending on whether the flags match
```

If you already have the objects in string form, just plug those strings into the `left` and `right` arguments. The function will return `true` if all relevant flags (optionality, privacy/protection, async, abstract, readonly, static) are considered identical, otherwise `false`.
